{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261027T134827Z-7367fad7", "ts": "2026-10-27T13:51:14Z", "actor": {"worktree": "prawduct", "plugin": "3.8.0-dev.1", "branch": "fix/1071-session-authored-span"}, "body": {"base_commit": "7c4fb9ff8a461cd0fce5f483b08360e1274892c7", "base_tree": "6cb6809cb1b9f43abf026c1a44bbf3a66328a6c3", "head_tree": "81ae14de105b72892f99084b2181b80336aac30c", "head_commit": "8449ed623d8f651893fa2c09e4f82ae64e488d83", "dispatch_commit": "8449ed623d8f651893fa2c09e4f82ae64e488d83", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md", "plugin/lib/briefing.py", "plugin/lib/gates.py", "tests/test_reflection_gate.py"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-session-authored-span.md", ".prawduct/changes/session-authored-span.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md", "plugin/lib/briefing.py", "plugin/lib/gates.py", "tests/test_reflection_gate.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-session-authored-span.md", ".prawduct/changes/session-authored-span.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "blocking", "title": "Merging a delegate's worktree branch now reads as imported history, so the Critic and reflection Stop gates go silent on delegated code", "recommendation": "HEAD's reflog is per worktree. A delegate dispatched with `isolation: \"worktree\"` (plugin/methodology/delegation.md: 'Take the branch and the report, merge it'; 'it builds, you govern \u2014 the Critic, reflection ... merges') commits in its own worktree, so its commits never show up as `commit:` entries in the coordinator's HEAD reflog. The coordinator's integration shows up only as `merge worktree-agent-<id>: Fast-forward` or `merge ...: Merge made by ...`. `_classify_reflog_subject` maps that to `imported`, the reflog is fully classified, and `_session_authored_paths` drops every delegated path. A chunk built by delegates and merged in this session therefore no longer arms the Critic Stop gate under an active plan, and it no longer arms the reflection gate. Before this change both gates fired. The same thing happens when the session merges its own branch that was built in another worktree. This is the dangerous direction: the docstring's 'a gate loses a false fire and never a real one' and the plan's 'Authority fails closed ... narrows on positive evidence' are both false here, because a `merge` subject is not evidence that the session did not author the work. Fix: decide authorship of merged and fast-forwarded history by something other than the reflog verb. One option is to count commits the move introduced (`git rev-list <pre-move>..<post-move>`) whose committer date falls at or after session start. Those are delegate commits and other in-session work. Pre-session upstream history still drops out, and anything ambiguous widens the span, which is the safe direction. Add a test that merges a branch committed after session start from a second worktree and asserts the span keeps its files, plus a Critic-guard counterpart to `test_a_pull_only_session_leaves_it_silent`.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py", "tests/test_reflection_gate.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "note", "title": "An empty HEAD reflog while HEAD's tree moved narrows away the session's committed work", "recommendation": "With `core.logAllRefUpdates=false`, or a deleted or expired HEAD log, `git reflog show HEAD` returns rc 0 with no lines. `commits` and `merges` stay empty and `authored` is just untracked files plus `diff HEAD`, so everything the session committed drops out of the span. That is a narrowing with no positive evidence behind it. Return None when HEAD's tree has moved but the walk found no entry at or after `started` (or reached no entry older than `started`). Pin this with a test.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "severity": "note", "title": "Rebase picks of commits made before the session count as this session's authored work", "recommendation": "Every pick-type `rebase (...)` entry is classified `authored`. Rebasing a feature branch onto develop replays commits from earlier sessions, so any path both those commits and upstream touched stays in the span and fires the gates. The error widens the span, which is the safe direction. Still, the change record's 'The session's own commits count, including rebased ones' overstates the precision. Name this residual false fire next to the shared-checkout one in the change record's consumer note, or bound picks by the original commit's committer date.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "severity": "note", "title": "Cost bound says 'one batched diff-tree', but a commit plus a hand-concluded merge costs two", "recommendation": "`_session_authored_paths` runs one `diff-tree` for `commits` (`-r --root`) and a separate one for `merges` (`--cc`). A session with both pays rev-parse, reflog, two diff-trees and a diff, which is four calls beyond the base diff, not the three that the docstring, the plan's Success line and the change record state. A pull-only session pays no diff-tree at all. State the bound as 'up to four'. Alternatively, if the cost line is meant to be pinned, add a commit-plus-merge tier to `test_its_git_calls_are_bounded_by_what_the_session_did`.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "Session start time gets a second source of truth (base-tree marker mtime) when a content-carried one already exists", "recommendation": "Instance. `_session_authored_paths` takes the start of the session from `stat().st_mtime` of `.session-base-tree`. But `_boundary_close_session` already writes `.session-start`, an ISO-8601 UTC timestamp written at the same boundary on the same not-brief_only path (prawduct-hook ~1197, before the anchors at ~1985), and older builds wrote it too. `gates._read_session_start` already reads it. So the plan's reason for using mtime ('a new marker field would need the session to be started by this plugin build') does not hold. Here is why it matters. File metadata moves when the file is copied, restored or touched, and a later mtime pushes the walk's lower bound forward. Session commits made before that bound then fall out of `commits` and get dropped from the span: the gate loses a real fire, which is the direction the docstring says can never happen ('a gate loses a false fire and never a real one'). It also leaves two answers to 'when did this session start' that can disagree. Fix: parse `_read_session_start(prawduct_dir)` to epoch seconds and use that as the bound. A missing or unparseable value returns None, as the mtime failure does now. Then the start time has one home, and it is written slightly earlier than the base-tree marker, which is the safe direction.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "The 'narrow only on a fully classified reflog' invariant is assumed, not built in: an empty or out-of-order reflog reads as 'nothing committed'", "recommendation": "Instance, in `_session_authored_paths`. The walk stops at the first entry whose stamp is earlier than `started`, and treats running out of reflog as a complete pass. It never shows that it reached the session boundary. Three states make it narrow with no positive evidence, and all three drop session commits: (a) the reflog is empty or missing for HEAD (`core.logAllRefUpdates=false`, or an expired or pruned reflog) even though HEAD's tree moved; (b) an in-session entry stamped earlier than the start, which stops the walk too soon. Stamps follow the committer date, as the test fixture's GIT_COMMITTER_DATE trick relies on, so clock skew or a committer-date override can cause it; (c) a reflog that begins after the session started. Fix: require the walk to reach an entry stamped before `started` and return None otherwise. Better still, also require the old value of the oldest in-session entry to resolve to `base`'s tree. Never `break` on a stamp that goes against reflog order. Add a test with a reflog that cannot reach the boundary, expecting the whole base-tree span. Then 'authority narrows only on positive evidence' (plan, architecture.md 'Authority fails closed') holds by construction rather than by assumption.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py", "tests/test_reflection_gate.py"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "Stop-hook budget/emitter comment still describes the old span and its cost", "recommendation": "Instance; prose that nothing reads. prawduct-hook ~3189-3193 still says `session_work_span` 'diffs the session's base tree against the working tree, so committed work is still this session's work' and that 'the call costs a `git diff`'. The span now drops imported history and can cost up to five more git calls. This changeset also changes what this gate sees: a pull that brings in someone else's learnings rule no longer sets `_rules_changed`. That is probably what you want, but the comment doesn't say so. Remedy: make the sentence relational ('reads the session's work span; see `session_work_span` for what it counts and costs') instead of restating the mechanism. No other edit is needed.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "Docstring cost counts say 'three more git calls' after rev-parse; the moved-HEAD path can make four", "recommendation": "Instance; an inert count. When a session has both ordinary commits and a hand-concluded merge, `_session_authored_paths` runs `diff-tree` twice (once with `-r --root`, once with `--cc`). The true count after `rev-parse` is reflog + up to two diff-tree + diff = three or four. The `session_work_span` docstring's 'one batched `diff-tree`' and the change record's 'three more' state the common case. No test or gate reads these counts, and no edit is wanted unless you're touching the docstring anyway.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py"], "fid": "R-8"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "Firing guard and Critic composition verdict now read different notions of the session's work", "recommendation": "Answers the plan's open question (Chunk 01 mid-build decision 3). This existed before the change, and the change makes it visible: the guard reads the authored span, but `session_review_verdict` still composes over base tree to working tree. A session that authors one file and also switches branches is therefore asked for coverage of the other branch's history. After a base pull, the merge-base fallback rescues it. After a branch switch it may not. File a backlog item scoped to the branch-switch case: base the verdict on the authored span's own base, or record that the fallback is the intended answer. It doesn't belong in this change. Scope: class (every verdict path that uses the base-tree marker as its start node).", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py"], "fid": "R-9"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "blocking", "title": "Merging this session's own delegate branch is classified as imported, so the gates go silent on delegated work", "recommendation": "_IMPORTED_SUBJECTS treats every `merge <branch>` reflog subject as history the session did not write. This repo's delegation workflow (worktree-isolated agents whose branches the integrating session merges back) produces exactly that subject. This repo's own HEAD reflog has more than 25 `merge worktree-agent-<id>` entries. When the merge is a fast-forward or an automatic merge, the delegate's commits sit only in the worktree's reflog, so _session_authored_paths drops their paths. A session whose code was written by its delegates then ends with the reflection gate and the Critic Stop gate both silent. That is a real fire lost, which contradicts the docstring's guarantee ('a gate loses a false fire and never a real one') and the plan's 'Authority fails closed' conformance claim. The classification decision never considered the delegation workflow. I escalated from WARNING under the Learnings Cross-Check, because the change reintroduces two warned-against patterns: 'When a fix NARROWS a detector, the verification set must include the TRUE POSITIVES it exists to catch' (every fixture is a merge of someone else's branch), and 'A pattern narrowed to kill a false positive was validated only on the case that PROVOKED it \u2014 re-run it over the whole corpus' (the plan's live check read one session, and a tally of this repo's reflog subjects shows the class). Scope: class. The sound direction is to stop treating `merge <branch>` as positive evidence of non-authorship unless the merged commits are provably not this session's, or to fall back to the full span for merges. Pin it with a fixture that merges a branch committed after the session started and expects its paths to stay in the span.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py", "tests/test_reflection_gate.py"], "fid": "R-10"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "Authorship giving up is silent: the #1071 false fire can return with nothing to attribute it to", "recommendation": "When HEAD's tree moved and _session_authored_paths returns None, the span is silently widened back to the base-tree diff, keeping the same `source: base-tree` value. The causes are an unrecognised subject, a failed reflog, a failed diff-tree or an unreadable mtime. Real subjects hit this path: this repo's reflog has `commit (cherry-pick)` and `Branch` entries, and neither is classified. In those sessions the user gets exactly the false block #1071 complains about, with no hint why. The next health survey also cannot tell 'narrowing skipped' from 'narrowing wrong'. The existing degraded case (`source == porcelain`) is named in the blocker text at prawduct-hook ~3105, so there is a precedent this path does not follow. Per 'Pick each fallback's direction from the invariant it protects: ... degraded advice names its consequence', the fallback direction is right but the consequence goes unnamed. Scope: instance. Make the give-up observable, for example with an additive span field or source value that the blocker and telemetry can read, plus a test asserting it on the unrecognised-subject fixture.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "fid": "R-11"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "Records Pass: the plan routes #1071's own repro to #282, which is dropped", "recommendation": "Records Pass, misleads-into-action bar. Covered set: .prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-session-authored-span.md and .prawduct/changes/session-authored-span.md. The plan's Out of scope says #1071's own repro (another session's commits in a shared checkout) belongs to '#282 (session lock) owns that seam'. The backlog cache resolves #282 as status=dropped, dead=True. The change record is titled '... (#1071)' as a fix, and the branch is fix/1071. A builder or merge step that marks #1071 shipped therefore leaves the residual that #1071's title names ('reflection span counts another session's commits') with no live owner, while the plan says one exists. The change record's consumer note correctly says that residual still counts. Scope: instance. Correct the plan's routing claim to match the backlog's actual state, and keep #1071's closure consistent with the fact that its stated repro is not fixed here.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-session-authored-span.md", ".prawduct/changes/session-authored-span.md"], "fid": "R-12"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Mid-build decisions and flagged inferences, for the owner", "recommendation": "Scope: none. The plan records four mid-build decisions, each with what settled it: (1) metadata paths are dropped before the authorship walk (cost goal); (2) the cost line was corrected to count the guarding rev-parse (do it right, pinned by test_its_git_calls_are_bounded_by_what_the_session_did); (3) only the firing span narrows, and the Critic composition verdict still composes base tree to working tree; (4) a live check against this repo, as the gates rule 'Editing a runtime that governs the current session ... run the changed detection against this repo first' requires. The plan flagged decision (3) for this review. The residual is real: a session that authored code and also pulled or switched branches still fires the Critic gate, and its verdict then composes coverage over the wider interval, including imported files, which can cost a review round for history the session did not write. Whether to track it is the builder's call. The flagged inferences (HEAD reflog as the authorship source, marker mtime as session start, fall back on anything unclassified) are recorded with alternatives and reasons. The exception is the `merge <branch>` classification, covered by the blocking finding above. No alignment-pass miss: the Goals cite #1071, the survey and the owner's go-ahead.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-session-authored-span.md"], "fid": "R-13"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Backlog reconciliation: #1071 is only partially addressed; no affected item is resolved", "recommendation": "Scope: none. The cache, confirmed 2026-10-27 and 43 minutes old, lists four open items affecting the changed files: #1079, #672, #755 and #928. None is resolved by this change. #1071 (open) is narrowed but not resolved, because its own title case (another session's commits in a shared checkout) is explicitly out of scope and still fires, as the change record's consumer note states. Verify before calling `/prawduct:backlog update 1071 status=shipped closed-by=scope:session-authored-span`. The backlog skill's 'When to mark shipped' rule is the test. Cited ids: #304 shipped, #1095 open, #282 dropped (see the Records Pass finding).", "fid": "R-14"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Learnings cross-check: read list and outcome", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Read list from `learnings-files --for-diff`: core.md, authoring.md, gates.md, hook-surface.md, release.md, tests.md. The escalations are folded into the blocking finding (tests.md 'When a fix NARROWS a detector...' and hook-surface.md 'A pattern narrowed to kill a false positive...') and the Goal 6 warning (hook-surface.md 'Pick each fallback's direction...'). Conforming: gates.md's 'run the changed detection against this repo first' (the live check is recorded); tests.md 'When a requirement is about a COST, assert the operation that costs' (the cost test asserts git calls per tier); tests.md 'When a deliberate change turns a passing test red, renegotiate the contract in the open' (TestTheKnownFalseFire was renamed and inverted with its reasoning). No rules were added or changed this cycle, and I found no implicit supersessions.", "fid": "R-15"}], "counts": {"blocking": 2, "warning": 4, "note": 9}, "observations": [], "duration_seconds": 420, "scope": "session-authored-span", "scope_chosen_by": "binding", "scope_unresolved_cause": null, "scope_reason": null, "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": ["01", "02"], "chunks_proposed": ["01", "02"], "chunk": "01", "base_reviewed": "7c4fb9ff8a461cd0fce5f483b08360e1274892c7", "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-session-authored-span.md", ".prawduct/changes/session-authored-span.md", "plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md"], "chunk_graded": "01", "chunks_graded": ["01", "02"], "plan_graded": "